<!--
  EXPERIMENTAL. Hart CPS combined core index, actual method: same lower
  and upper thermal wind and closed-low mask as cps_HCPSclass.xml,
  squashed into a single continuous number from -3 (cold core) to +3
  (deep warm core) via 2*tanh(HVTL/scaleM) + tanh(HVTU/scaleM) - see
  cps_HartCPS.executeIndexStd and D2D/README.md's "Joint classification
  and index" section.

  TWELVE METHOD BLOCKS, tried in order, with the same structure as
  cps_HCPSclass.xml (band set as the outer loop, MSLP input as the inner;
  see that file's comment for why the two choices are multiplied out):
    Methods 1-4   cps_HartCPS.executeIndex7: Hart's own 900-600 and
                  600-300 hPa bands at 50 hPa spacing (thirteen GH
                  Fields, 600 hPa once). Runs on a model that carries
                  every 50 hPa level from 900 to 300 hPa (the GFS).
    Methods 5-8   cps_HartCPS.executeIndexMean2: the standard levels -
                  the lower term is the mean of the 925/850/700 and
                  850/700/500 hPa slopes, the upper 500/400/300 hPa.
                  Runs on every other model that carries the six
                  standard levels.
    Methods 9-12  cps_HartCPS.executeIndexStd: the 925/850/700 lower band
                  this package shipped with, same inputs as methods 5-8;
                  the documented fallback, reached only if methods 5-8
                  (or 1-8) are removed.
  Within each group of four the MSLP input is MSLP at MSL, PMSL at MSL,
  MSLP at Surface, then GH at 1000MB with pmslKind 1.

  Why MSLP for the closed-low mask (cps_HartCPS.closed_low_mask): it is
  one field, the same surface the forecaster already contours, so the
  closed low the mask finds is the closed low drawn on the chart; it is
  defined everywhere, so there is no extrapolation below sea level
  inside a deep low (as there is for 1000 hPa height) and nothing to
  mask below ground; and the ring test reads directly in pressure: the
  annulus 300 to 500 km out must average at least depthHpa (5 hPa)
  above the candidate center, which must itself be within 0.6 hPa of
  the lowest MSLP within 300 km. Pa or hPa are both accepted (the same
  median rule as the surface pressure field).

  Below-ground masking: same as cps_HCPSclass.xml - every height Field
  below (the band levels) is blanked (NaN) before its own computation
  wherever it is below ground for its own pressure, using the surface
  pressure field (P, Surface) and capHpa - see
  cps_HartCPS.mask_below_ground. capHpa (default 900 hPa) is a cap, not
  the literal level pressure: it keeps a deep low's own legitimately low
  surface pressure over open water from being mistaken for terrain,
  while still masking the Greenland ice sheet and Iceland highlands at
  925/900/850 hPa - see cps_HCPSclass.xml's comment for the full
  explanation. The MSLP Field is not masked; the index is blanked over
  terrain because HVTL is.

  VERIFY: same checks as cps_HCPSclass.xml (GH abbreviation, fixed-level
  Field spelling including the 50 hPa levels in the GFS inventory, P
  abbreviation and its Surface level spelling, dx/dy pseudo-fields and
  ConstantField support, levels= value, unit="" acceptance), and in
  addition:
    - the mean sea level pressure Field spellings. Neither the PMSL
      abbreviation nor the MSL level spelling could be verified at
      first install; MSLP at MSL and PMSL at MSL have since loaded on
      GFS and ECMWF at OPC. If the definition fails to load or comes back
      empty for a model, try level="0.0MSL", then omitting the level
      attribute, then the model's own MSLP abbreviation (some models
      carry reduced MSLP under a model-specific name rather than PMSL),
      as a further block in each group. The same change applies to
      cps_HCPSclass.xml.
-->
<DerivedParameter abbreviation="HCPSidx" name="Hart CPS Index -3 to +3" unit="">
    <Method name="cps_HartCPS.executeIndex7" levels="Surface">
        <!-- Method 1: Hart's seven-level bands (GFS); MSLP input: the baseline MSLP derived parameter (the Volume Browser "MSL Press"), which aliases each model's own field -->
        <Field abbreviation="MSLP" level="MSL"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="900MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="850MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="800MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="750MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="700MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="650MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="600MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="550MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="500MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="450MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="400MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="350MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="GH" level="300MB"/>
        <Field abbreviation="P" level="Surface"/>
        <Field abbreviation="dx"/>
        <Field abbreviation="dy"/>
        <!-- radiusKm (km): Hart's own 500 km analysis-circle radius, used for both thermal winds and as the mask's ring_radius_km (the outer edge of the annulus the depth test averages over) -->
        <ConstantField value="500.0"/>
        <!-- scaleM (m): tanh squashing scale - roughly the |HVTL|/|HVTU| magnitude that reads as fully warm/cold -->
        <ConstantField value="100.0"/>
        <!-- depthHpa (hPa): the annulus around a candidate point must average at least this much higher MSLP than the point itself before it counts as a real closed low, not a uniform slope -->
        <ConstantField value="5.0"/>
        <!-- blobKm (km): radius of the blob painted around each detected low's center (a display radius, not the low's own true physical size) -->
        <ConstantField value="200.0"/>
        <!-- capHpa: below-ground cap, see cps_HartCPS.py -->
        <ConstantField value="900.0"/>
        <!-- pmslKind: 0 = the first Field is mean sea level pressure (Pa or hPa), 1 = it is 1000 hPa height, converted at 8 m per hPa -->
        <ConstantField value="0.0"/>
    </Method>
